%[text] %[text:anchor:T_52C4D0C5] # Quadcopter Basics - Configuration and Modeling
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] In [PropellerModel](file:PropellerModel.m), we learned how the thrust and torque generated by a propeller depend on both its physical dimensions and its rotational speed. In this script, we'll put these concepts together to understand how a quadcopter with *four* propellers works and run simple simulations to illustrate as we go along. 
%[text] For simplicity, we will assume a rigid-body dynamic model with four propellers that all produce unidirectional thrust perpendicular to the quadcopter system. Throughout this script, we compute the parameters needed to implement a moving quadcopter in Simulink in [QuadcopterMovement](file:QuadcopterMovement.m).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4AF4F552] ## Quadcopter Coordinate System
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_3156] ![](text:image:83bc)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Image: [Quadcopter Physical Characteristics](https://www.mathworks.com/help/aeroblks/quadcopter-physical-characteristics.html)
%[text] Conventionally, we define the coordinate system (the body frame) for a quadcopter using the right-hand rule, with the x-axis pointing 'forward,' the y-axis pointing to the right, and the z-axis pointing downwards. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7DD3BADC] ### Propeller Configuration
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_4b16] ![x-configuration quadcopter of radius R and propeller radius r. The +x direction is forward, the +y direction is right, the propellers are numbered 1-4, clockwise, starting with the front-left propeller. Adjacent propellers rotate in opposite directions.](text:image:195d) ![+-configuration quadcopter of radius R and propeller radius r. The +x direction is forward, the +y direction is right, the propellers are numbered 1-4, clockwise, starting with the front propeller. Adjacent propellers rotate in opposite directions.](text:image:7535)
%[text]{"align":"center"}     'x' Configuration                                                                                     '+" Configuration                               
%[text] For this course, we will consider the quadcopter being operated in one of two modes:
%[text] - an 'x' configuration where the propellers are offset diagonally by 45 degrees to the direction of the quadcopter's main body.
%[text] - a '+' configuration, where the propellers are aligned along the x and y axes (or east-west/north-south).  \
%[text] In each configuration, every propeller is placed at an offset of 90 degrees from the next, with its center being a distance *R* away from the body's center of mass. Each blade has a radius of *r*, and can spin at a specified angular velocity *w*. In each configuration, we set propellers 1 and 3 to spin counterclockwise (as seen from above) while propellers 2 and 4 spin clockwise. This counter-rotation will be important [later](internal:M_85a3). We'll call the structure connecting our four propellers the *body*.
%%
%[text] ## Motivating example
%[text] Recall how we modeled a propeller as a disk? Even a *very basic* physical model that describes a quadcopter as four such disks and a centralized body mass lets one predict:
%[text] - How arm length $R${"altText":"R"} affects roll/pitch authority and inertia.
%[text] - How prop radius $r${"altText":"r"} and prop mass $m${"altText":"m"} influence attitude responsiveness.
%[text] - How propeller spin directions produce yaw torque.
%[text] - Why x-configuration drones are more common than +-configuration drones. \
%[text] This is the same modeling pipeline used for early-stage controller design before adding aerodynamic drag, motor dynamics, and sensor models.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3F0D6EF8] ### Simple Geometric Model
%[text] We'll use a [body frame](internal:M_3156) with $x\_B${"altText":"x\_B"} pointing forward, $y\_B${"altText":"y\_B"} pointing right, and $z\_B${"altText":"z\_B"} pointing down. As a first approximation, let's assume that the propellers are thin disks of radius $r${"altText":"r"} meters and mass $m${"altText":"m"} kilograms, while the body is a cuboid of length $L${"altText":"L"} in the $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} directions and height $H${"altText":"H"} in the $z${"altText":"z"} direction. Let the mass of the body be $M\_{\\text{body}}${"altText":"M\_(body)"}. That gives the total mass of the quadcopter as $M\_{\\text{quad}} = 4m+M\_{\\text{body}}${"altText":"M\_(quad) = 4m+M\_(body)"}. Placing the origin at the center of mass of the quadcopter, and with propeller arms of length $R${"altText":"R"} in the plane $z=0${"altText":"z=0"}, this positions each propeller as follows:
%[text:table]
%[text] | <p align="center"> Propeller</p> | <p align="center">+-configuration</p> | <p align="center">Prop</p><p align="center">Location</p> | <p align="center">x-configuration</p> | <p align="center">Prop</p><p align="center">Location</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | <p align="center">1</p> | <p align="center">$(+R,0,0)${"altText":"(+R,0,0)"}</p> | <p align="center">front</p> | <p align="center">$\\left(+\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)${"altText":"(+(R)\/(sqrt(2)),-(R)\/(sqrt(2)))"}</p> | <p align="center">front-left</p> |
%[text] | <p align="center">2</p> | <p align="center">$(0,+R,0)${"altText":"(0,+R,0)"}</p> | <p align="center">right</p> | <p align="center">$\\left(+\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}},+\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)${"altText":"(+(R)\/(sqrt(2)),+(R)\/(sqrt(2)))"}</p> | <p align="center">front-right</p> |
%[text] | <p align="center">3</p> | <p align="center">$(-R,0,0)${"altText":"(-R,0,0)"}</p> | <p align="center">rear</p> | <p align="center">$\\left(-\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}},+\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)${"altText":"(-(R)\/(sqrt(2)),+(R)\/(sqrt(2)))"}</p> | <p align="center">rear-right</p> |
%[text] | <p align="center">4</p> | <p align="center">$(0,-R,0)${"altText":"(0,-R,0)"}</p> | <p align="center">left</p> | <p align="center">$\\left(-\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{R}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)${"altText":"(-(R)\/(sqrt(2)),-(R)\/(sqrt(2)))"}</p> | <p align="center">rear-left</p> |
%[text:table]
m = 0.07;  % propeller mass %[control:slider:2814]{"position":[5,9]}
Mbody = 1.02;                    % body mass [kg]
  %[control:button:98c5]{"position":[1,2]}
Mquad = 4*m + Mbody              % quadcopter mass 
%%
%[text] ### **Simple Inertia Model** 
%[text] From the work in [Propeller Model](file:PropellerModel.m), we know that quadcopter propellers rotate quickly. As such, it is physically reasonable to a first approximation to model each propeller as a thin solid disk of radius $r${"altText":"r"} and mass $m${"altText":"m"}. For simplicity, we will approximate the body of the quadcopter as a cuboid with side lengths $L${"altText":"L"} in the $x${"altText":"x"}-$y${"altText":"y"} plane, and height $H${"altText":"H"} in the $z${"altText":"z"} direction. 
%[text] Recall that the moment of inertia tensor in 3D space is $I = \\left\[\\matrix{I\_{xx} & I\_{xy} & I\_{xz} \\cr I\_{yx} & I\_{yy} & I\_{yz} \\cr I\_{zx} & I\_{zy} & I\_{zz}}\\right\]${"altText":"I = \[matrix with rows (I\_(xx), I\_(xy), I\_(xz)); (I\_(yx), I\_(yy), I\_(yz)); (I\_(zx), I\_(zy), I\_(zz))\]"}. Inertia describes how difficult (or easy) it is to change the orientation of our quadcopter, impacting flight stability, agility, and performance.
%[text] ### Inertia of Propellers
%[text] About its own center (with disk normal aligned with $z\_B${"altText":"z\_B"}):
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:65c6) $I\_{\\text{disk, center}} = \\left\[\\matrix{\\frac{1}{4}mr^2 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & \\frac{1}{4}mr^2 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}mr^2}\\right\]${"altText":"I\_(disk, center) = \[matrix with rows ((1)\/(4)mr^2, 0, 0); (0, (1)\/(4)mr^2, 0); (0, 0, (1)\/(2)mr^2)\]"}.
%[text] The [parallel axis theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_axis_theorem) states that the moment of inertia of an object with mass $m${"altText":"m"} around an axis parallel to one through the center of mass, a distance $R${"altText":"R"} away, adds $mR^2${"altText":"mR^2"} to the inertia through the center of mass. More formally, if $p${"altText":"p"} is the vector from the center of mass of the quadcopter to the center of the respective propeller: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_{\\text{about CoM}} = I\_{\\text{center}} + m\\left(||p||^2\\left\[\\matrix{1 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1}\\right\] - p^{\\top}p\\right)${"altText":"I\_(about CoM) = I\_(center) + m(||p||^2\[matrix with rows (1, 0, 0); (0, 1, 0); (0, 0, 1)\] - p^(transpose)p)"}.
%[text] For propeller 1 in the +-configuration, with $p = \[\\matrix{R& 0 & 0}\]${"altText":"p = \[matrix with rows (R, 0, 0)\]"}, this becomes:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_{\\text{Prop 1 about CoM}} = \\left\[\\matrix{\\frac{1}{4}mr^2 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & \\frac{1}{4}mr^2 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}mr^2}\\right\] + m\\left(\[\\matrix{R & 0 & 0}\]\\cdot\\left\[\\matrix{R \\cr 0 \\cr 0}\\right\]\\left\[\\matrix{1 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1}\\right\] - \\left\[\\matrix{R \\cr 0 \\cr 0}\\right\]\\cdot \[\\matrix{R & 0 & 0}\]\\right)${"altText":"I\_(Prop 1 about CoM) = \[matrix with rows ((1)\/(4)mr^2, 0, 0); (0, (1)\/(4)mr^2, 0); (0, 0, (1)\/(2)mr^2)\] + m(\[matrix with rows (R, 0, 0)\] times \[matrix with rows (R); (0); (0)\]\[matrix with rows (1, 0, 0); (0, 1, 0); (0, 0, 1)\] - \[matrix with rows (R); (0); (0)\] times \[matrix with rows (R, 0, 0)\])"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_{\\text{Prop 1 about CoM}} = \\left\[\\matrix{\\frac{1}{4}mr^2 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & \\frac{1}{4}mr^2 + mR^2 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}mr^2+ mR^2}\\right\]${"altText":"I\_(Prop 1 about CoM) = \[matrix with rows ((1)\/(4)mr^2, 0, 0); (0, (1)\/(4)mr^2 + mR^2, 0); (0, 0, (1)\/(2)mr^2+ mR^2)\]"} 
%[text] By symmetry, this means that for the +-configuration, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_{\\text{all props about CoM}} = \\left\[\\matrix{mr^2 + 2mR^2 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & mr^2+2mR^2 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 2mr^2+4mR^2}\\right\]${"altText":"I\_(all props about CoM) = \[matrix with rows (mr^2 + 2mR^2, 0, 0); (0, mr^2+2mR^2, 0); (0, 0, 2mr^2+4mR^2)\]"}.
%[text] ### Inertia of Body
%[text] The body inertia is simplest to express when the edges of the cuboid align with the $x${"altText":"x"}, $y${"altText":"y"}, and $z${"altText":"z"} axes: in the x-configuration. 
%[text] In this case, the inertia of the body for the x-configuration is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_{\\text{x-body}} = \\left\[\\matrix{\\frac{M\_{\\text{body}}}{12}(L^2+H^2) & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & \\frac{M\_{\\text{body}}}{12}(L^2+H^2) & 0 \\cr  0 & 0 & \\frac{M\_{\\text{body}}}{6}(L^2)}\\right\]${"altText":"I\_(x-body) = \[matrix with rows ((M\_(body))\/(12)(L^2+H^2), 0, 0); (0, (M\_(body))\/(12)(L^2+H^2), 0); (0, 0, (M\_(body))\/(6)(L^2))\]"}.
%[text] In the +-configuration, the body cuboid is rotated by $\\theta = 45^{\\circ}${"altText":"theta = 45^(degrees)"} in the $x${"altText":"x"}-$y${"altText":"y"} plane from this version. That means applying a rotation matrix of
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $R = \\left\[\\matrix{\\cos(\\theta) & -\\sin(\\theta) & 0 \\cr \\sin(\\theta) & \\cos(\\theta) & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1}\\right\] = \\left\[\\matrix{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\cr \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1}\\right\]${"altText":"R = \[matrix with rows (cos(theta), - sin(theta), 0); (sin(theta), cos(theta), 0); (0, 0, 1)\] = \[matrix with rows ((sqrt(2))\/(2), -(sqrt(2))\/(2), 0); ((sqrt(2))\/(2), (sqrt(2))\/(2), 0); (0, 0, 1)\]"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_{\\text{+-body}} = RI\_{\\text{x-body}}R^{\\top}${"altText":"I\_(+-body) = RI\_(x-body)R^(transpose)"}
%%\ Mass + inertia for "+" quad (very simple)
m = 0.07;  % propeller mass   [kg] %[control:slider:6a46]{"position":[5,9]}
Mbody = 1.02; % body mass [kg] %[control:slider:52a2]{"position":[9,13]}
L = 0.17; % body length [m] %[control:slider:9033]{"position":[5,9]}
H = 0.06; % body height [m] %[control:slider:8cc3]{"position":[5,9]}
Rval = 0.13; % arm length   [m] %[control:slider:197b]{"position":[8,12]}
r = 0.12; % propeller radius [m] %[control:slider:073e]{"position":[5,9]}

if Rval <= r
    warning("You must choose Rval > r or the quadcopter isn't physically feasible.")
    Rval = r+0.01;
end

if L >= 2/sqrt(2)*Rval
    warning("Why is your body extending beyond your arms?")
end

syms R
assume(R,"real")
% propeller positions ("+")
 propPlus = [ +R,  0, 0;
    0, +R, 0;
    -R,  0, 0;
    0, -R, 0];

% propeller positions ("x")
 propX = [R/sqrt(2) -R/sqrt(2) 0;...
      R/sqrt(2) R/sqrt(2) 0; ...
      -R/sqrt(2) R/sqrt(2) 0; ...
      -R/sqrt(2) -R/sqrt(2) 0];

configuration = "+"; %[control:dropdown:8965]{"position":[17,20]}
if configuration == "+"
    pB = double(subs( propPlus,R,Rval));
elseif configuration == "x"
    pB = double(subs( propX, R, Rval));
end

M = 4*m + Mbody;    % total mass (4 props + body)

I3 = eye(3);
I_disk_center = diag([ (1/4)*m*r^2, (1/4)*m*r^2, (1/2)*m*r^2 ]);

Iprop = zeros(3,3);
for i = 1:4
    p = pB(i,:);
    Iprop = Iprop + (I_disk_center + m*((p*p.')*I3 - (p.'*p)));
end

Ibody = diag([(1/12)*Mbody*(L^2+H^2) (1/12)*Mbody*(L^2+H^2) (1/12)*Mbody*(L^2+L^2)]);
if configuration == "+"
    theta = 45;
    RotationMatrix = [cosd(theta) -sind(theta) 0; sind(theta) cosd(theta) 0; 0 0 1];
    Ibody = RotationMatrix*Ibody*RotationMatrix.';
end

  %[control:button:96da]{"position":[1,2]}
I = Iprop+Ibody;

disp("Total mass M [kg] =") 
disp(M)
disp("Inertia matrix I [kg*m^2] =")
disp(I)
%[text] Pulling out the individual values, this gives:
Ixx = I(1,1)
Iyy = I(2,2)
Izz = I(3,3)
%[text] ![Lightbulb](text:image:8310) **Reflect**. What parts of this computation change as you switch between configurations?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_85a3] ## **Thrust and yaw-drag model**
%[text] A simple and common thrust model is quadratic in propeller speed:
%[text] - Thrust from propeller $i${"altText":"i"}:  \
%[text]{"align":"center"} $T\_i = k\_T \\omega\_i^2${"altText":"T\_i = k\_T omega\_i^2"}
%[text] - Reaction (drag) yaw torque from propeller $i${"altText":"i"}:  \
%[text]{"align":"center"} $Q\_{z,i} = \\epsilon\_i k\_Q \\omega\_i^2${"altText":"Q\_(z,i) = epsilon\_i k\_Q omega\_i^2"}
%[text] where $k\_T${"altText":"k\_T"} and $k\_Q${"altText":"k\_Q"} are coefficients, $\\omega\_i${"altText":"omega\_i"} is the speed of rotation of the $i${"altText":"i"}th propeller, and $\\epsilon\_i = +1${"altText":"epsilon\_i = +1"} for counterclockwise (CCW) propellers and $-1${"altText":"-1"} for clockwise (CW) propellers.
%[text] We’ll use a standard alternating spin pattern where propellers 1 and 3 are CCW, and 2 and four are CW, as shown in the [original figures](internal:M_4b16):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\epsilon = \\left\[\\matrix{+1 \\cr -1 \\cr +1 \\cr -1}\\right\]${"altText":"epsilon = \[matrix with rows (+1); (-1); (+1); (-1)\]"}
%[text] ![Lightbulb](text:image:99b4) **Reflect**. What would happen to your torque if you spun all the propellers in the same direction? What does that do to the quadcopter?
%%
%[text] ### Derive the Mixer
%[text] The mixer, $A${"altText":"A"}, is a matrix that encodes the thrust and torque generated by the rotations of each propeller. Thus, $A${"altText":"A"} provides a mapping for a model to convert rotational speed into force.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\left\[\\matrix{T\\cr Q\_x \\cr Q\_y \\cr Q\_z}\\right\] = A\\left\[\\matrix{\\omega\_1 \\cr \\omega\_2 \\cr \\omega\_3 \\cr \\omega\_4}\\right\]${"altText":"\[matrix with rows (T); (Q\_x); (Q\_y); (Q\_z)\] = A\[matrix with rows (omega\_1); (omega\_2); (omega\_3); (omega\_4)\]"}.
%[text] The total thrust magnitude is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $T = \\sum\_i T\_i = k\_T \\sum\_i \\omega\_i^2${"altText":"T = sum over i of T\_i = k\_T sum over i of omega\_i^2"}
%[text] Since $z\_B${"altText":"z\_B"} is **down**, thrust points in the upward or $-z\_B${"altText":"-z\_B"} direction. Thus, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $T\_i = \\left\[\\matrix{0 \\cr 0 \\cr -T\_i}\\right\]${"altText":"T\_i = \[matrix with rows (0); (0); (-T\_i)\]"}.
%[text] With $p\_i${"altText":"p\_i"} denoting the vector from the center of the quadcopter to the hub of the $i${"altText":"i"}th propeller, the torque from the thrust is 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $Q = \\sum\_i p\_i \\times T\_i${"altText":"Q = sum over i of p\_i cross T\_i"}
%[text] Using $p\_i = \\left\[\\matrix{x\_i \\cr y\_i \\cr 0}\\right\]${"altText":"p\_i = \[matrix with rows (x\_i); (y\_i); (0)\]"}, this becomes $p\_i\\times T\_i = \\left\[\\matrix{-y\_iT\_i \\cr +x\_iT\_i\\cr 0}\\right\]${"altText":"p\_i cross T\_i = \[matrix with rows (-y\_iT\_i); (+x\_iT\_i); (0)\]"}. 
syms R k_m k_T
epsilon = [1 -1 1 -1];
pB = propX.'; %[control:dropdown:1848]{"position":[6,11]}
A = [k_T*ones(1,4); 
    -pB(2,:)*k_T;
    pB(1,:)*k_T;
    epsilon*k_m]
syms omega_1 omega_2 omega_3 omega_4 T Q_x Q_y Q_z
omega = [omega_1^2; omega_2^2; omega_3^2; omega_4^2];
  %[control:button:5600]{"position":[1,2]}
displayFormula("[T; Q_x; Q_y; Q_z] == A*omega")
%[text] ![Lightbulb](text:image:8ca1) **Reflect**. What parts of this computation change as you switch between configurations?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E3AD4162] ### Estimate Constants
%[text] The thrust produced is modeled as a function of the propeller's angular velocity and the propeller's diameter. Under [standard modeling techniques for a quadcopter propeller](file:PropellerModel.m), the thrust $T${"altText":"T"} and moment $Q${"altText":"Q"} can be approximated as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $T\_{\\text{prop}} \\propto \\rho \\omega^2 r^4 = \\rho \\left(\\frac{N}{60}\\right)^2 r^4${"altText":"T\_(prop) is proportional to rho omega^2 r^4 = rho ((N)\/(60))^2 r^4"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $Q\_{\\text{prop}} \\propto \\rho\\omega^2 r^5  = \\rho \\left(\\frac{N}{60}\\right)^2 r^5${"altText":"Q\_(prop) is proportional to rho omega^2 r^5 = rho ((N)\/(60))^2 r^5"}
%[text] Where $\\rho${"altText":"rho"}{"editStyle":"visual"} is the air density at the given altitude, N is the angular speed of the propeller in rpm, and $r${"altText":"r"} is the radius of the propeller. Let $M${"altText":"M"} be the mass of the quadcopter body and $m${"altText":"m"} be the mass of each propeller. In this case, the thrust will exactly balance the weight of the quadcopter when:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $4\\cdot T\_{\\text{prop}} = (M+4m)\\cdot g${"altText":"4 times T\_(prop) = (M+4m) times g"}.
%[text] Ordinarily, this requires taking some measurements of real motors and propellers. For the sake of our model, let's assume that this thrust is achieved with $N = 4000${"altText":"N = 4000"}.
g = 9.81; % m/s^2

% Quadcopter weight
W = (Mbody+m*4)*g
% for single propeller
T_prop = W/4
N = 4000 % rpm - nominal value
  %[control:button:1df3]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] Then 
kT = T_prop/N^2
%[text] From experimental data \[1\], we assume a value for $k\_m${"altText":"k\_m"} with
%[text]{"align":"center"} $Q\_{\\text{z}} = k\_mN^2${"altText":"Q\_(z) = k\_mN^2"}
%[text]  as $k\_m/k\_T \\approx 10^{-2}${"altText":"k\_m\/k\_T approximately 10^(-2)"}:
km = 0.01*kT
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3613A551] #### Performance Curves - Torque & Moment vs Speed
%[text] This gives the performance curves of the propellers as seen below:
N = 0:1000:10000; % sweeping speed values from 0 - 10,000 rpm
T_sweep = kT * N.^2;
M_sweep = km * N.^2;
  %[control:button:4cce]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] Plotting:
subplot(2,1,1)
plot(N, T_sweep)
xlabel('Speed (rpm)')
ylabel('Thrust (N)')
grid minor

subplot(2,1,2)
plot(N, M_sweep)
xlabel('Speed (rpm)')
ylabel('Moment (N.m)')
grid minor
%[text] These numbers will give us an easy reference for future calculations or design decisions.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A78E948A] ## Forces and Torques
%[text] Using the assumptions we made above, let's model how the propeller forces and the propeller moments act on the quadcopter. 
%[text] ![Lightbulb](text:image:322e) **Reflect**. What parts of this computation change as you switch between configurations?
%[text] The propellers are numbered 1 through 4, and opposite propellers (1/3 and 2/4) rotate in the same direction to cancel out their reaction moments. In a 'hover' condition, the total thrust produced by all propellers should cancel out the weight exactly, and the rotational moments should all cancel out to zero. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:82e0)
%[text] 
  %[control:button:4a84]{"position":[1,2]}
quad_m = double(subs(A,[k_T k_m R],[kT km Rval]))
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_134d] In a steady state, where all the propellers are spinning at the nominal speed (say, 4000 rpm), all the moments cancel each other out, leaving a residual force. 
N = [4000 4000 4000 4000] 
f = quad_m*N'.^2
%[text] We can check that the total thrust force is equal to the weight of the quadcopter, as per our design. By convention, the thrust force acts in a direction opposite to the weight, thereby allowing the quadcopter to hover.
  %[control:button:9fb3]{"position":[1,2]}
f(1) - W
%[text] Any change from this condition will lead to movement of one type or another, which is explained in detail in the next script: [Quadcopter Movement](file:QuadcopterMovement.m)
%%
%[text] ## Save the Parameter Values
%[text] We can also save the above parameters in the `Data` folder for reuse.
  %[control:button:53c1]{"position":[1,2]}
rootPath = currentProject().RootFolder;
if any(quad_m == 0)
    filename = "plus_quad_params.mat";
else
    filename = "x_quad_params.mat";
end
w = [0 0 0 0]; % Initial rotation speeds
fileloc = fullfile(rootPath,"Data",filename);
save(fileloc,"M","m","R","r","Ixx","Iyy","Izz","quad_m","W","kT","km","g","w")
disp("The file has been saved to " + newline + fileloc)
%%
%[text] ## Next Steps
